/**

@page benchmark_multigrid_petsc_hdg Mixed Possion equation with PETSc

Here we discuss the steps involved in running the file benchmarks/multigrid/petsc/hdg.py.
We will try and discuss some of the mathematics behind the solver, but we mainly focus on
how to build and run it.

For information on how to build Peano with PETSc, see \ref multigrid_home.

Mixed Poisson / Hybridised Discontinuous Galerkin
======================


At time of writing, the actual focus of this part of the repo has not yet been fixed. We
aim to provide a fairly general solver, which we describe here.

## Cell and Face Degrees of Freedom

Taking some inspiration from DG schemes, we fix the number of degrees of freedom on the 
cells and the faces by the degree of our desired polynomial. In particular, for 
polynomal degree @f$ n @f$ and spatial dimension @f$ d @f$, we set up our equation to 
have:

@f$  (n + 1) ^d  @f$ degrees of freedom on each face (hereafter referred to as cellDofs), of which
@f$  ( n + 1 )^ {d-1}  @f$ degrees of freedom coincide with each face (hereafter referred to as faceDofs).


## What does the code do?

Much of the setup is hidden from the user. 

We set up each cell as follows:
\li In each dimension we have @f$ n+1 @f$ basis polynomials
\li In each dimension we have @f$ n+1 @f$ quadrature points

We promote the 1 dimensional basis to a d-dimensional basis by multiplying together basis functions.
For instance in two dimensions, if a quadrature point is @f$ i @f$ units along the x axis, and @f$ j @f$
units along the y axis, then we create a 2d basis function by multiplying together the basis function 
for position @f$ i @f$ with the basis function for position @f$ j @f$.

For each cell, we send a square matrix into the global PETSc matrix. This local matrix will have
@f$  (n + 1) ^d  @f$ rows. We evaluate the element for local matrix at position @f$  (i,j)  @f$
by taking the integral across the cell for (d-dimensional) basis functions @f$ i @f$ and @f$ j@f$, 
using a Gaussian quadrature method. See petsc.actionsets.AssemblePETSCMatrix.AssemblePETSCMatrixWithQuadratures
for implementation details. 

We also constrain the Dof-points along each face to match the Dof-points along a neighbouring face. This
is something of a departure from true DG.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

## The python code itself

We create a project:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
project = petsc.Project(project_name = "HDG", 
                        namespace = [ "benchmarks", "multigrid", "petsc", "hdg" ]
                        ) 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This just sets up our namespaces. The real information comes in the next steps.

We add in some global parameters:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cube_size = 1.0
project.set_global_simulation_parameters(
  dimensions            = args.dimensions,
  offset                = [ 0.0       for _ in range(args.dimensions) ],
  domain_size           = [ cube_size for _ in range(args.dimensions) ],
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and we add in a solver:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver = petsc.solvers.HdgFirstDiscretisation("HDG",
                                              args.degree,
                                              cellDofs,
                                              faceDofs,
                                              args.meshsize,
                                              args.meshsize,
                                              args.dimensions,
                                              )


project.add_solver( solver )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

This will copy all the code in, and ensure that the PETSc routines are available. These PETSc 
routines are customisable, but for now this is out of the scope of this tutorial.

## Building the project

All the user needs to do is invoke the python script, with build style and polynomial degree:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 hdg.py -m release --degree 1
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


*/